; nec_ir.pe — a NEC infrared remote frame, sent by firmware.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS ONE, AND WHAT IT IS ACTUALLY TESTING
;
; Three of the acts in this block (WS2812, DHT11, DS18B20) drive a wire where
; a receiver RESYNCHRONISES: the WS2812 cell is a fixed 800 ns and the DHT11
; window is a fixed 45 us, and in both the receiver decides by LEVEL, so a
; program that is one clock out still talks to a real part. An infrared remote
; is the opposite and is the sharpest thing in this repository to model: there
; is no wire. The only thing that leaves this pin is LIGHT, and a NEC receiver
; recovers the data from a 38 kHz burst it has to find, integrate and time.
; Get the carrier wrong and the frame does not arrive; get a burst or a gap
; wrong and the receiver decodes a different button.
;
; So the number that is the claim is the CARRIER PERIOD, measured on the pin,
; clock by clock, and the frame is decoded from what the pin actually emitted.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PROTOCOL, AND EVERY PLACE A NUMBER HAD TO BE FITTED
;
;   leader      9 ms of carrier
;   leader gap  4.5 ms of silence
;   bit 0       0.5625 ms burst + 1.6875 ms of silence
;   bit 1       0.5625 ms burst + 0.5625 ms of silence
;   stop        one final 0.5625 ms burst
;
; The bits are LSB FIRST, like 1-Wire and unlike every other serial protocol
; here. The frame is 32.06 ms for one byte: 9 + 4.5 + 8 x 2.25 + 0.5625.
;
; EVERY DURATION IN THIS PROGRAM IS AN INSTRUCTION COUNT, and none of them is
; read from the 1 us tick. The tick's phase residual is up to a full
; microsecond, which is 7.6 % of a carrier half period -- and a carrier is the
; one thing in this repository that cannot be a little bit late. So the carrier
; is fitted to 38.0 kHz to the CLOCK (a half period is 789 or 790 clocks) and
; the silences are fitted to the coarse (4,40) counter pair, exactly as
; servo_sweep.pe and dht11_read.pe fit their slots.
;
; The bursts are counted in CARRIER CYCLES rather than in time, which is both
; what a real transmitter does and the only way the two numbers stay
; consistent: 342 cycles and 21 cycles of the same carrier ARE the 9 ms and the
; 0.5625 ms, so a period error cannot make the frame internally inconsistent.
; The TB then checks each burst against its own datasheet window, so a program
; that got one of the two counts wrong is still caught.
;
; A BURST LONGER THAN 255 CYCLES NEEDS TWO COUNTERS, and this one does. The
; leader is 342 carrier cycles and the ISA's LDI immediate is EIGHT BITS, so
; 342 could not be written in one instruction at all: it truncated to 86, and
; the frame that left the pin was a well-formed 38 kHz carrier carrying a
; 2.3 ms "9 ms" leader. Nothing about it looked wrong. So the count is
; IR_LEADR runs of IR_LEAD cycles, and the reload costs nine instructions once
; per run -- 0.0017 % of a leader and 0.03 % of a data burst, both far inside
; the datasheet windows, and it lands on the FIRST cycle of a run, which is the
; burst's initiation rather than a carrier half period.
;
; THAT TRUNCATION IS THE KIND OF DEFECT THIS BLOCK KEEPS FINDING: it is the
; same shape as the WS2812 byte counter that started at 0 and the DHT11
; accumulator that read "ADD A,X" as a load. An eight-bit field is not wide
; enough for the number you want, and nothing downstream complains.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TWO HALF-PERIOD CONSTANTS ARE SEPARATE, AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANCY
;
; The delay routine returns to its caller through a phase DISPATCH -- a ladder
; of LDM/SUB/JZ -- so the number of instructions between the end of one delay
; and the start of the next depends on WHICH RUNG the ladder stops at. The two
; carrier half periods therefore cost a different number of instructions and
; are fitted separately (IR_H1 and IR_H2) to make the two halves equal. This
; is the same "equalised branches" defect the WS2812 act found twice: with one
; constant the carrier came out as an alternating 37.9/38.1 kHz, which is a
; program that is a fraction of a clock out on every other edge, and a window
; check forgives it. The carrier's two phases are also deliberately the FIRST
; two rungs of the ladder, so the cost is as close to free as it can be.
;
; bit 6 is the IR LED: the first unclaimed pad in the documented map, and the
; same pad the other timing acts use because they are alternatives rather than
; additions to one image.

        ; the pin and the fitted constants are peasm CONSTS: IR_DATA, IR_H1,
        ; IR_H2, IR_LEAD, IR_BIT, IR_GAP0, IR_GAP1, IR_LEADGAP. See
        ; tools/fw/peasm.py's timing block for the values and the arithmetic.

; ===========================================================================
; CLAIM THE PIN. The LED is driven, not open-drain: the cathode is on the pin
; and the anode is on the supply, so the LED is ON when the pin sinks. That is
; the opposite convention from the 1-Wire act, and the reason is electrical:
; 1-Wire's high level belongs to a pull-up, this one's belongs to the supply.
; ===========================================================================
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; released: nothing is emitting
        LDI   A, 0x00
        STM   12, A             ; the phase the delay returns to
        LDI   A, 0x00
        STM   14, A             ; the "frame sent" flag, and the TB's observable
        JMP   ir_send_leader

; ===========================================================================
; THE LEADER: IR_LEADR runs of IR_LEAD carrier cycles, which is 342 of them
; and so 9 ms at the fitted period
; ===========================================================================
ir_send_leader:
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   6, A              ; "when the burst ends, go to phase 2"
        LDI   A, IR_LEADR
        STM   1, A              ; ...in this many runs of IR_LEAD cycles
        LDI   A, IR_LEAD
        STM   7, A              ; ...of this many carrier cycles each
        JMP   ir_emit

; ===========================================================================
; TURN THE LED ON. Every burst -- the leader, a data burst, the stop burst --
; comes through here, and that is the point of it having one copy.
;
; The pin has to be RE-DRIVEN after every gap, because a gap is made by
; releasing the pin and a burst is made by driving it again. The first version
; drove it once, at the leader, and let the data bursts inherit that: the
; program then ran the whole frame -- eight bits, the right gaps, the right
; carrier, the stop burst -- with the pad released for every burst except the
; leader and the last one. It emitted a 9 ms leader, 13.6 ms of silence, and
; one 0.5625 ms burst, and the TB's decode was empty. Three copies of "drive
; the pin" would have been three places to keep in step; the duplication this
; removes was written as a comment warning about exactly that risk.
; ===========================================================================
ir_emit:
        LDI   A, IR_DATA
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; drive: the burst is on the wire from here
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; LOW: sinking, so the LED is emitting
        JMP   ir_burst

; ===========================================================================
; THE CARRIER LOOP
;
; The pin is already driven and low on entry. One iteration is one carrier
; cycle: a low half period, a high half period, and the cycle count. The count
; reaching zero ends the burst, and dmem[6] says which end that is. No
; register is touched that another part of the frame is using: dmem[7] is the
; cycle count and dmem[6] is the burst's end kind, and the delay owns 9-15.
; ===========================================================================
ir_burst:
ir_cyc:
        LDI   A, IR_DATA
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; the low half period is over: drive HIGH
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 0
        LDI   A, IR_H1
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 44
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 0: the high half period is over; sink again ----
ph0:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; LOW
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 1
        LDI   A, IR_H2
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 34
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 1: the low half period is over; that is one carrier cycle ----
;
; The count is TWO levels because of the eight-bit immediate: a burst longer
; than 255 cycles is several runs of at most 255, and the reload lands on the
; first cycle of a run.
ph1:    LDM   A, 7              ; the cycles left in this run
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   7, A
        JNZ   ir_cyc           ; more cycles in this run
        LDM   A, 1              ; this run is done: how many are left?
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   1, A
        JZ    ir_burst_end      ; the burst is over
        LDI   A, IR_LEAD        ; another run: reload the cycle count
        STM   7, A
        JMP   ir_cyc

; ---- the burst is over. dmem[6] says which end this is.
;
; The values are 0, 1, 2 because the ladder below ENUMERATES them, and a
; ladder that does not cover its own input domain is the same defect three
; times in this repository: dmem[6] held 2/3/4 (the phase numbers themselves,
; which is the obvious thing to write) and the ladder treated anything past 1
; as the stop burst, so the frame ended after the leader with a well-formed
; carrier and nothing in it. A dispatch is a FUNCTION over the values the
; program actually produces; using the phase numbers as the enum hides the
; mismatch until the day someone reorders the phases.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------
ir_burst_end:
        LDM   A, 6
        JZ    ph2               ; 0: the leader
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph3               ; 1: a data bit
        JMP   ph4               ; 2: the stop burst

; ===========================================================================
; PHASE 2: the leader burst is over -- 4.5 ms of silence
; ===========================================================================
ph2:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; released: the gap is silence
        LDI   A, 6
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 6
        LDI   A, IR_LEADGAP
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 3              ; the (3,130) pair, not (4,40): see below
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 130
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

ph6:    JMP   ir_send_byte

; ===========================================================================
; SEND ONE BYTE: eight bits, LSB FIRST
;
; dmem[0] is the byte and is never modified -- it is the record the TB
; compares against what it decoded off the wire, so it must be the ORIGINAL
; and not something the program derived. dmem[3] is the rotating copy,
; dmem[2] the bits left, dmem[4] the bit just sent, dmem[6] which end of a
; burst it is, dmem[1]/[7] its two-level cycle count. The bit is the copy's own
; bit 0 and the copy is rotated right after each slot, so there is no shifting
; to get wrong -- the same idiom the 1-Wire write path uses, for the same
; reason.
;
; This block was, for a while, unreachable: it sat after a JMP with nothing
; pointing at it, so the bit counter was never set, the loop ran 255 times
; instead of eight, and the frame that left the pin was a valid carrier with a
; valid-looking burst train and the wrong number of slots in it. Falling into
; a setup block is the cheap way to write one and the expensive way to miss
; one.
;
; The payload is 0xA5: both bit values in the byte and six transitions in
; eight, and neither its bit-reverse (0xA5 reversed is 0xA5) nor its
; complement (0x5A) is anything a wrong bit order produces by accident.
; ===========================================================================
ir_send_byte:
        LDI   A, 0xA5
        STM   0, A              ; the byte, unmodified for the TB to read
        STM   3, A              ; the rotating copy
        LDI   A, 8
        STM   2, A              ; eight bits

; ---- start one bit: the byte's bit 0 is the bit to send, then the burst.
; The bit is taken BEFORE the burst's counters are set, and the block ends in
; a jump to the carrier -- the other order is a loop that never emits, which
; is what it was for a while.
ir_start_bit:
        LDM   A, 3
        AND   A, 0x01
        STM   4, A              ; ...and the gap will be chosen from it
ir_data_burst:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   6, A              ; a data burst ends at phase 3
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   1, A              ; one run of IR_BIT cycles
        LDI   A, IR_BIT
        STM   7, A              ; 21 carrier cycles = 0.5625 ms
        JMP   ir_emit

; ===========================================================================
; PHASE 3: a data burst is over. THE GAP LENGTH IS THE BIT: 1.6875 ms for a
; zero, 0.5625 ms for a one, and a receiver reads the whole frame off that
; alone. A transmitter that sent the same gap for both would emit a frame that
; is well formed and carries nothing.
; ===========================================================================
ph3:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; released: silence until the next burst
        LDM   A, 4              ; the bit that was just sent
        JNZ   ph3_one

        LDI   A, 5              ; a ZERO: the long gap, then phase 5
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, IR_GAP0
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 4
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 40
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

ph3_one:
        LDI   A, 5              ; a ONE: the short gap -- about one burst --
        STM   12, A             ; ...and STILL phase 5
        LDI   A, IR_GAP1
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 4
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 40
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ===========================================================================
; PHASE 5: a gap is over, and what happens next depends only on how many bits
; are left. The gap does NOT have to remember which kind of gap it was -- the
; next burst is the next burst either way -- so both kinds return to the same
; phase. Two phases would have been wrong, not merely redundant: the phase
; number is the delay routine's only return address, and a second one would
; mean a second copy of the code that starts a burst, which is a second place
; for the two copies to disagree.
; ===========================================================================
ph5:    LDM   A, 3              ; the byte being sent
        SHR   A                 ; the next bit of the frame is now bit 0
        STM   3, A
        LDM   A, 2              ; the bits left in this byte
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   2, A
        JZ    ir_stop           ; all eight sent: the stop burst next
        JMP   ir_start_bit

; ===========================================================================
; THE STOP BURST
;
; The eighth bit's gap is followed by one final 0.5625 ms burst, which is what
; tells a receiver that the frame ended rather than that the remote is
; transmitting a very long gap. It is the part of a frame a firmware written
; for "send the byte" usually leaves out, and the TB counts the carrier cycles
; in it rather than checking only that something was emitted.
; ===========================================================================
ir_stop:
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   6, A              ; the stop burst ends at phase 4
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   1, A              ; one run
        LDI   A, IR_BIT
        STM   7, A
        JMP   ir_emit

; ===========================================================================
; PHASE 4: the stop burst is over. Raise the flag and park. dmem[0] already
; holds the byte that was sent, so there is nothing to bank.
; ===========================================================================
ph4:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; released: the frame is over
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   14, A             ; the frame is out
        LDI   A, IR_LEAD
        STM   9, A              ; ...and then the program is through, and the
        LDI   A, 4              ;     leader's own counters are dead. Not a
        STM   10, A             ;     loop: this is a counted delay, so a
        LDI   A, 40              ;     silent park rather than a spin.
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1
park:   LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A
halt:   JMP   halt              ; the ISA has no HALT

; ===========================================================================
; THE DELAY -- the same three nested counters as every other act here, with
; the same reload trap in it.
;
;     total(n1,n2,n3) = (n1-1) * (10 + (n2-1) * (4*n3 + 7)) + 4   clocks
;
; The carrier half periods use DIFFERENT (n2,n3) pairs, one for each half,
; because the delay routine returns through the phase ladder and the two rungs
; cost a different number of instructions. Both are fitted so that the two
; halves come out EQUAL, which is the property that matters and the reason
; there are two constants: with one constant the carrier alternated 37.9/38.1
; kHz, a program that is out by a fraction of a clock on every other edge.
; The (2,44) pair has a 193-clock outer step and the (2,34) pair a 153-clock
; one; the ladder costs 7 clocks more on the way out of phase 1 than out of
; phase 0, and the two step sizes are what absorb that.
;
; Everything else -- the leader's gap and both data gaps -- uses (4,40), where
; one outer step is 511 clocks = 8.52 us, which is the right resolution for
; windows of 562 us, 1687 us and 4500 us.
;
; THE DISPATCH IS A FUNCTION OVER EVERY PHASE THE PROGRAM USES, and it is
; written against the full set 0..6 rather than patched in as phases were
; added. The ISA cannot return to seven addresses from one place, so the phase
; in dmem[12] chooses where, and the carrier's two phases are deliberately the
; first two rungs of the ladder: every rung costs two instructions, and the
; carrier's half periods have to pay for all of them.
; ===========================================================================
dly1:   LDM   A, 9              ;  1  the outer counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  2
        STM   9, A              ;  3
        JZ    dly_done          ;  4
        LDM   A, 10             ;  5  the middle counter's target
        STM   11, A             ;  6   into the loop's working copy
dly2:   LDM   A, 11             ;  7  the middle counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  8
        STM   11, A             ;  9
        JZ    dly1              ; 10
        LDM   A, 13             ; 11  the inner counter's target
        STM   15, A             ; 12   into the loop's working copy
dly3:   LDM   A, 15             ; 13  the inner counter
        SUB   A, 1              ; 14
        STM   15, A             ; 15
        JNZ   dly3              ; 16
        JMP   dly2              ; 17
dly_done:
        LDM   A, 12
        JZ    ph0               ; 0: the high half period is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph1               ; 1: the low half period is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph2               ; 2: the leader burst is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph3               ; 3: a data burst is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph4               ; 4: the stop burst is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph5               ; 5: a gap is over
        JMP   ph6               ; 6: the leader's gap is over
